# What a NodeDB reset leaves behind, and how the client says so.
#
# The radio's database holds 80 entries and evicts; ours outlives the connection on purpose,
# because a node pushed out of the radio's list often exists nowhere else. Reset the radio's
# database and the two counts part company - the Status screen reads the radio's, the Nodes tab
# reads ours - which is the moment this scene is about.
#
# `offradio all` stands in for the reset itself: the request goes out over the air and the
# answer comes back on the next sync, and there is no radio behind the harness to do either.
# The scene stops at the confirm sheet for the same reason the keyboard scenes stop at START:
# forgetting is mesh_app's to carry out, and there is no app here to do it.

scene demo
tab nodes
hold 900

# The reset lands. Every node but our own is one only this Brick remembers now.
offradio all
hold 1800

# Both counts on one screen: the radio's database, and what this Brick still holds.
tab status
hold 1800

# Settings > Radio actions. The row that clears them says how many there are, and sits under
# the reset that left them behind.
tab settings
key down 12
key a
hold 900
key down 3
hold 1600
key a
hold 2200
